(167603, 9124, 3032, '<td valign="top">~~~~Critique Club Comment~~~~<br/> <br/>*Composition (content)*<br/>I loved this shot right away, as I am a sucker for urban landscapes and long exposure roads with car lights. The challenge is easily met, that is not an issue. The rules were broad and the sky is blue, but more important is the nice blue reflection on the snow on the edged of the roof and the ground.<br/>As I already said in my previous comment: Nice perspective.<br/>Altought the title say''s that The Drake is the main subject, the eye is drawn to the streets with traffic. I see an urban artery, a city alive.<br/>You could say that the building at the right spoils it a bit and that it should be cropped out, but on the other hand it gives a glimp of live behind those windows. It still has some detail because of a good exposure.<br/>The only dissapointment is that the red lights in the right lanes are overblown by all the white light from the uncoming traffic, but there is not much you can do about that.<br/> <br/>*Background*<br/>Nice sky with some clouds, horizon is visible because of the lights.<br/> <br/>*Camera Work (Technical)*<br/>Horizon looks straight enough. The building on the right distorts somwhat, I guess because of a wideangle lens setting, not much can be done about that from this position I think.<br/>Exposure is good, there is detail in the shadow of the building at the right, the snow isn''t blown out. Good depth of field. Sharpness ok.<br/> <br/>*Digital Processing (technical)*<br/>Looks good. I noticed that the file is only 100k. Try to save it at a higher quality, to keep sharpness and detail up. The limit is 150kb. It doesn''t hurt the picture here, but keep it in mind.<br/> <br/>*My opinion*<br/>I like it!<br/>And I really need to go to Chicago one day. I studied Chicago''s urban history for America Studies at the Amsterdam University, there is so much architecture to photograph there. I''ll add it to my favorites. ;-)<br/></td>', '~~~~Critique Club Comment~~~~ *Composition (content)*I loved this shot right away, as I am a sucker for urban landscapes and long exposure roads with car lights. The challenge is easily met, that is not an issue. The rules were broad and the sky is blue, but more important is the nice blue reflection on the snow on the edged of the roof and the ground.As I already said in my previous comment: Nice perspective.Altought the title say''s that The Drake is the main subject, the eye is drawn to the streets with traffic. I see an urban artery, a city alive.You could say that the building at the right spoils it a bit and that it should be cropped out, but on the other hand it gives a glimp of live behind those windows. It still has some detail because of a good exposure.The only dissapointment is that the red lights in the right lanes are overblown by all the white light from the uncoming traffic, but there is not much you can do about that. *Background*Nice sky with some clouds, horizon is visible because of the lights. *Camera Work (Technical)*Horizon looks straight enough. The building on the right distorts somwhat, I guess because of a wideangle lens setting, not much can be done about that from this position I think.Exposure is good, there is detail in the shadow of the building at the right, the snow isn''t blown out. Good depth of field. Sharpness ok. *Digital Processing (technical)*Looks good. I noticed that the file is only 100k. Try to save it at a higher quality, to keep sharpness and detail up. The limit is 150kb. It doesn''t hurt the picture here, but keep it in mind. *My opinion*I like it!And I really need to go to Chicago one day. I studied Chicago''s urban history for America Studies at the Amsterdam University, there is so much architecture to photograph there. I''ll add it to my favorites. ;-)', '2002-12-09 11:45:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(167624, 9250, 2764, '<td valign="top">Sigh - I thoguht (I still think) this picture was a ten!  I like the post processing too.  Is this a sign that American patriotism is out?  Did it lose points  because of the title?  What happened?</td>', 'Sigh - I thoguht (I still think) this picture was a ten!  I like the post processing too.  Is this a sign that American patriotism is out?  Did it lose points  because of the title?  What happened?', '2002-12-09 11:56:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(167643, 9029, 5436, '<td valign="top">Awesome shot. Congrats!</td>', 'Awesome shot. Congrats!', '2002-12-09 12:05:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(167656, 8928, 2764, '<td valign="top">His eyes are definately blue, but this picture doesn''t make them look very pretty, does it?  If I were your dad I would be mad that you made me look so old.  But I think the oldness is what makes it a good picture.  The focus is very nice, and even thought you used a flash, the colors came out good.  Sometimes the flash makes the colors look washed out or pale.  The light is a bit too bright down on his cheek  There is a lot of detail in your picture, every little hair shows up, I like the way his eyebrows curl.<br/> <br/>To make the picture better, I think the compostiton could have been better.  It might be more interesting if the eyeball was looking at the viewer.   But then, of course your Dad would have had to look right into the flash and you might have blined him. and gotten "red eye"....so maybe that''s not a good idea.   How about if you had remembered the rule of thirds and put the eyebal just a bit lower in the picture.  I think that would have made it more interesting because the human brain likes to look at  things that are about a third of the way on a picture.   Yours is one third from the right but it is centered from top to bottom.<br/> <br/>A nice portrait of your Dad.  Keep up the good work.</td>', 'His eyes are definately blue, but this picture doesn''t make them look very pretty, does it?  If I were your dad I would be mad that you made me look so old.  But I think the oldness is what makes it a good picture.  The focus is very nice, and even thought you used a flash, the colors came out good.  Sometimes the flash makes the colors look washed out or pale.  The light is a bit too bright down on his cheek  There is a lot of detail in your picture, every little hair shows up, I like the way his eyebrows curl. To make the picture better, I think the compostiton could have been better.  It might be more interesting if the eyeball was looking at the viewer.   But then, of course your Dad would have had to look right into the flash and you might have blined him. and gotten "red eye"....so maybe that''s not a good idea.   How about if you had remembered the rule of thirds and put the eyebal just a bit lower in the picture.  I think that would have made it more interesting because the human brain likes to look at  things that are about a third of the way on a picture.   Yours is one third from the right but it is centered from top to bottom. A nice portrait of your Dad.  Keep up the good work.', '2002-12-09 12:15:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(167661, 9029, 772, '<td valign="top">Jacko, congrats on first with an excellent shot :)</td>', 'Jacko, congrats on first with an excellent shot :)', '2002-12-09 12:19:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(167664, 8948, 772, '<td valign="top">marbo, congrats on 3rd :)  This is an interesting shot.. I was just curious if the ''star'' effect is natural or if you used a star filter on this shot... great work :)</td>', 'marbo, congrats on 3rd :)  This is an interesting shot.. I was just curious if the ''star'' effect is natural or if you used a star filter on this shot... great work :)', '2002-12-09 12:20:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(167683, 9066, 2764, '<td valign="top">I went with the crowd and gave your picture a 5.  The color is nice and the focus is exceptionally fine.  Perhaps it would have done better in the stopped motion challenge.  Overall its not a very captivating picture.  Obviously is it water but it is impossible to tell where the water is coming from and where it is going.  The dry area on the upper left is particularly confusing since at first glance it looks like a deep pool that the stream is falling into.  What is going on?<br/> <br/>Composition wise this does not fit into the categories that the human brain seems to find pleasing.  The main focus of the picture is the water, which is centered left right, and where it hits the pool is dead center.  Perhaps if  you had tried the rule of thirds.  I would have put it in the upper left third and taken more advantage of the ripples that the water makes.  Also my brain likes to follow the lines in a picture.  An effective composition had some sort of pleasing flow.  Here there is a strong sense of movement from the top to the center where it goes pffft.  Again if it had been moved to the left and up a bit, I ould follow the water in from the left and the ripples out to the right.  Of course, you don''t HAVE to follow the "rules" and sometimes breaking the rules makes a striking image.  I don''t think your image is strong enough to stand as a rule breaker.<br/> <br/>The glare on the upper left is distracting, was it a flash?  Overall I think you should repeat the shot,  Maybe you have an out take that is better?  you have done a wonderful job of capturing flowing water and the color of the picture is just lovely.  The picture could have been sooo much better if it had been framed differently and the ripple emphasized.  Could have been a winner instead of falling dead center in the voting range.</td>', 'I went with the crowd and gave your picture a 5.  The color is nice and the focus is exceptionally fine.  Perhaps it would have done better in the stopped motion challenge.  Overall its not a very captivating picture.  Obviously is it water but it is impossible to tell where the water is coming from and where it is going.  The dry area on the upper left is particularly confusing since at first glance it looks like a deep pool that the stream is falling into.  What is going on? Composition wise this does not fit into the categories that the human brain seems to find pleasing.  The main focus of the picture is the water, which is centered left right, and where it hits the pool is dead center.  Perhaps if  you had tried the rule of thirds.  I would have put it in the upper left third and taken more advantage of the ripples that the water makes.  Also my brain likes to follow the lines in a picture.  An effective composition had some sort of pleasing flow.  Here there is a strong sense of movement from the top to the center where it goes pffft.  Again if it had been moved to the left and up a bit, I ould follow the water in from the left and the ripples out to the right.  Of course, you don''t HAVE to follow the "rules" and sometimes breaking the rules makes a striking image.  I don''t think your image is strong enough to stand as a rule breaker. The glare on the upper left is distracting, was it a flash?  Overall I think you should repeat the shot,  Maybe you have an out take that is better?  you have done a wonderful job of capturing flowing water and the color of the picture is just lovely.  The picture could have been sooo much better if it had been framed differently and the ripple emphasized.  Could have been a winner instead of falling dead center in the voting range.', '2002-12-09 12:35:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(167691, 9154, 656, '<td valign="top">A good attempt and certainly a worthwhile composition and strong graphic image.  Certainly worth trying again to fix the technical issues.  <br/> <br/>You might want to underexpose it slightly more to increase the colour saturation, then try ''bringing'' it back up in software - should get you crisper colours, perhaps.   <br/> <br/>You really need to find a way to illuminate the front of the lemon, either by a much brighter rear light or some very directed spot lighting, perhaps a torch carefully played on it might help.  Not sure that the stem is properly lined up with the background, should be possible to get a really clean ''split'' right down the stem.  <br/> <br/>The reflections on the glass could do with some more careful control too - it is best to do these in a completely dark room, with any obvious light coloured or reflecting surfaces removed or covered up - you want everything behind the camera to be dark or nonreflective if you can, or mask off the ''stage'' with black foam core.  This has a lot of potential still to be realised.</td>', 'A good attempt and certainly a worthwhile composition and strong graphic image.  Certainly worth trying again to fix the technical issues.   You might want to underexpose it slightly more to increase the colour saturation, then try ''bringing'' it back up in software - should get you crisper colours, perhaps.    You really need to find a way to illuminate the front of the lemon, either by a much brighter rear light or some very directed spot lighting, perhaps a torch carefully played on it might help.  Not sure that the stem is properly lined up with the background, should be possible to get a really clean ''split'' right down the stem.   The reflections on the glass could do with some more careful control too - it is best to do these in a completely dark room, with any obvious light coloured or reflecting surfaces removed or covered up - you want everything behind the camera to be dark or nonreflective if you can, or mask off the ''stage'' with black foam core.  This has a lot of potential still to be realised.', '2002-12-09 12:40:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(167712, 8872, 2764, '<td valign="top">Oh, I''m glad I got assigned to review this picture.  As it loaded SLOWLY on my dial up connection I thought "Yep, here''s the winner!"  But then it kept loading all that extra street scene and I was disappointed.  I don''t think I can add much to what everyine else has said.  This is almost a stunning picture and I hope you will recrop it and post it somewhere where we can see it again.<br/> <br/>The clouds, the towering bank, the glint of sunshine are visially beautiful and the make such a statement.    It looks like a church doesn''t it?  The modern day icon of what we worship, powerful and imposing and even kissed by the sun.  Like a stack of coins propped up an each side by dollars, or whatever bills are used to prop up banks in Bahrain.  What a statement for our times.<br/> <br/>So take out the scissors and cut it right along that wire thing on the right and take away ALL the street scene, it is too dark anyways.  Then clone out the streetlight (yes it''s cheating but the challenge is over and you get to make it perfect now).  Perhaps t could be brightened up a bit without losing the beautiful blue sky?  maybe not.  ANd then send it to Fortune Magazine to use as a cover!  <br/> <br/>This is one great shot and it really deserves to be fixed up a bit!  Other than the cropping, the camera angle and the focus and the light, all the photographic decisions were well made.  It''s all there to make it perfect.<br/> <br/>from the Critique Club<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2002-12-10 08:14:29.</i></td>', 'Oh, I''m glad I got assigned to review this picture.  As it loaded SLOWLY on my dial up connection I thought "Yep, here''s the winner!"  But then it kept loading all that extra street scene and I was disappointed.  I don''t think I can add much to what everyine else has said.  This is almost a stunning picture and I hope you will recrop it and post it somewhere where we can see it again. The clouds, the towering bank, the glint of sunshine are visially beautiful and the make such a statement.    It looks like a church doesn''t it?  The modern day icon of what we worship, powerful and imposing and even kissed by the sun.  Like a stack of coins propped up an each side by dollars, or whatever bills are used to prop up banks in Bahrain.  What a statement for our times. So take out the scissors and cut it right along that wire thing on the right and take away ALL the street scene, it is too dark anyways.  Then clone out the streetlight (yes it''s cheating but the challenge is over and you get to make it perfect now).  Perhaps t could be brightened up a bit without losing the beautiful blue sky?  maybe not.  ANd then send it to Fortune Magazine to use as a cover!   This is one great shot and it really deserves to be fixed up a bit!  Other than the cropping, the camera angle and the focus and the light, all the photographic decisions were well made.  It''s all there to make it perfect. from the Critique Club', '2002-12-09 12:52:16.000000', '2002-12-10 08:14:29.000000', 0),
(167727, 9206, 2764, '<td valign="top">PS - Bet it would have won the Motion Challenge!!</td>', 'PS - Bet it would have won the Motion Challenge!!', '2002-12-09 13:03:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(167735, 9218, 1019, '<td valign="top">Critique Club<br/> <br/> <br/>This composition of this photo is deceptively simple. The large bow catches the eye instantlysuch a recognisable object and then with the great contrast between the blue ribbon and the white of the package your eye is firmly held by the ribbon.You have used the rule of thirds for your composition and it is very appropriate for a present. The huge contrast between the dark blue of the ribbon and bow and the gift wrap is very eyecatchingand added to it the shiny nature of the material  this makes for an eyepopping picture.<br/> <br/>There is no technical information but I would guess that you used either a flash or direct lighting from above as the shadows are quite hard. The lighting has slightly overexposed the wrap as there is no textureperhaps you removed all colours apart from blueas the slight blue shadow near the ribbon shows. I think you probably oversaturated the blue as the colour is harsh. But the glitter and reflections are shown clearly.<br/> <br/>But the whole point of this picture and what gives it its great appeal is the universal emotion we all have when we look at a beautifully wrapped presentwe love it- we want to hold it-- to open it  . We all have memories of great gifts and this evokes in the viewer those memories. The simplicity of your picture puts nothing in the way of the viewer and their memories. Well done you have evoked the child in all of us.<br/> <br/>Andrew<br/> <br/></td>', 'Critique Club  This composition of this photo is deceptively simple. The large bow catches the eye instantlysuch a recognisable object and then with the great contrast between the blue ribbon and the white of the package your eye is firmly held by the ribbon.You have used the rule of thirds for your composition and it is very appropriate for a present. The huge contrast between the dark blue of the ribbon and bow and the gift wrap is very eyecatchingand added to it the shiny nature of the material  this makes for an eyepopping picture. There is no technical information but I would guess that you used either a flash or direct lighting from above as the shadows are quite hard. The lighting has slightly overexposed the wrap as there is no textureperhaps you removed all colours apart from blueas the slight blue shadow near the ribbon shows. I think you probably oversaturated the blue as the colour is harsh. But the glitter and reflections are shown clearly. But the whole point of this picture and what gives it its great appeal is the universal emotion we all have when we look at a beautifully wrapped presentwe love it- we want to hold it-- to open it  . We all have memories of great gifts and this evokes in the viewer those memories. The simplicity of your picture puts nothing in the way of the viewer and their memories. Well done you have evoked the child in all of us. Andrew ', '2002-12-09 13:07:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(167746, 8960, 4493, '<td valign="top">~~~~Critique Club Comment~~~~<br/>On Line<br/> <br/>*Composition (content)*<br/>The composition of this shot really works. The contrast of the white clothes line against the fabulous blue is perfect. Even the clothes pins orientation work, they move the eye from left to right and add interest to the top of the shot. The quilts location and angle is good as the right side of the quilt lines up almost perfect with the edge of the picture.<br/> <br/>*Background*<br/>Not much there :-) The color of the sky and its gradient from dark to light is very nice. The sky is clean and free of noise.<br/> <br/> <br/>*Camera Work (Technical)*<br/>The only slight drawback I see technically is that you may have let just a little to much light in. The white on the quilt in a few areas seems just a tad too white.<br/> <br/>*Digital Processing (technical)*<br/>Sharpening is OK (slight trace of over sharpening at the bottom of the quilt).<br/>Balance between light and dark is excellent.<br/>Compression is good (no jpeg artifacts)<br/> <br/>*My opinion*<br/>I liked this picture during voting and still like it. It tells a story and meets the challenge real well. Keep up the good work!<br/> <br/></td>', '~~~~Critique Club Comment~~~~On Line *Composition (content)*The composition of this shot really works. The contrast of the white clothes line against the fabulous blue is perfect. Even the clothes pins orientation work, they move the eye from left to right and add interest to the top of the shot. The quilts location and angle is good as the right side of the quilt lines up almost perfect with the edge of the picture. *Background*Not much there :-) The color of the sky and its gradient from dark to light is very nice. The sky is clean and free of noise.  *Camera Work (Technical)*The only slight drawback I see technically is that you may have let just a little to much light in. The white on the quilt in a few areas seems just a tad too white. *Digital Processing (technical)*Sharpening is OK (slight trace of over sharpening at the bottom of the quilt).Balance between light and dark is excellent.Compression is good (no jpeg artifacts) *My opinion*I liked this picture during voting and still like it. It tells a story and meets the challenge real well. Keep up the good work! ', '2002-12-09 13:10:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(167760, 8995, 3018, '<td valign="top">Congratulations on your 5th place!  It was a great shot!</td>', 'Congratulations on your 5th place!  It was a great shot!', '2002-12-09 13:18:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(167768, 8948, 4517, '<td valign="top">Thank you all for your comments. I didn`t expect to do this well in the challenge and i`m well pleased with 3rd place.<br/>Quite a few of you have asked if i used a filter to get the star effect. I wish i could say i did, but it was just a fluke caused by the flash and the many strip lights overhead. I did little to the photo apart from crop and levels. Thank you all for your votes. Marbo.</td>', 'Thank you all for your comments. I didn`t expect to do this well in the challenge and i`m well pleased with 3rd place.Quite a few of you have asked if i used a filter to get the star effect. I wish i could say i did, but it was just a fluke caused by the flash and the many strip lights overhead. I did little to the photo apart from crop and levels. Thank you all for your votes. Marbo.', '2002-12-09 13:22:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(167780, 9008, 4493, '<td valign="top"><b>~~~~Critique Club Comment~~~~</b> <br/>Ice Age<br/> <br/>*Composition (content)*<br/>Your composition and use of color and light are good. As one of the other comments mentions, a center of focus would be good. Something to draw the eye to. As it is now, there is just so much happening that it''s somewhat chaotic. Your concept was very good. Definitely get a sense of blue.<br/> <br/>*Background*<br/>Your DOF is good. Again the background seems very busy to the eye. I wonder if a different aperture so as to blur out the back a little would help maybe F4.<br/> <br/> <br/>*Camera Work (Technical)*<br/>Good use of the camera to get a close-up macro. <br/> <br/>*Digital Processing (technical)*<br/>Sharpening is ok (trace of over sharpening around foreground ice).<br/>Balance between light and dark is good.<br/>Compression is good (no jpeg artifacts)<br/> <br/>*My opinion*<br/>I liked this picture during voting. I am a fan for abstract work and you did a good job. Other than not having a center of focus this shot is very well done.</td>', '~~~~Critique Club Comment~~~~ Ice Age *Composition (content)*Your composition and use of color and light are good. As one of the other comments mentions, a center of focus would be good. Something to draw the eye to. As it is now, there is just so much happening that it''s somewhat chaotic. Your concept was very good. Definitely get a sense of blue. *Background*Your DOF is good. Again the background seems very busy to the eye. I wonder if a different aperture so as to blur out the back a little would help maybe F4.  *Camera Work (Technical)*Good use of the camera to get a close-up macro.  *Digital Processing (technical)*Sharpening is ok (trace of over sharpening around foreground ice).Balance between light and dark is good.Compression is good (no jpeg artifacts) *My opinion*I liked this picture during voting. I am a fan for abstract work and you did a good job. Other than not having a center of focus this shot is very well done.', '2002-12-09 13:27:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(167788, 9154, 258, '<td valign="top">Very nice Jim!</td>', 'Very nice Jim!', '2002-12-09 13:29:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(167833, 8997, 3942, '<td valign="top">Hi Gordon, I really like your shot, one of my four 10s this week too. IMHO a little more DOF would be suitable and I would like to see the light-reflection in the center of both eyes. Doing so makes the shot a bit more lifely. Nevertheless congrats on a great shot!</td>', 'Hi Gordon, I really like your shot, one of my four 10s this week too. IMHO a little more DOF would be suitable and I would like to see the light-reflection in the center of both eyes. Doing so makes the shot a bit more lifely. Nevertheless congrats on a great shot!', '2002-12-09 13:48:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(167837, 9002, 5250, '<td valign="top">Yes, this is a picture of BLUE silly putty. I had just gotten my camera (first one ever) and wanted to at least enter something in the current contest! I threw this shot together without a tripod and without proper lighting. The shot was created by placing a blue towel over top of the power bar I had sitting on my dresser and adding the silly putty to the scene. Not bad for a first attempt, but I will have better things to offer soon I hope! ;)</td>', 'Yes, this is a picture of BLUE silly putty. I had just gotten my camera (first one ever) and wanted to at least enter something in the current contest! I threw this shot together without a tripod and without proper lighting. The shot was created by placing a blue towel over top of the power bar I had sitting on my dresser and adding the silly putty to the scene. Not bad for a first attempt, but I will have better things to offer soon I hope! ;)', '2002-12-09 13:50:58.000000', NULL, 0),
